[2026-03-15T01:32:31.703Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GP4Jok71KQr3sdrzSKSerZ","type":"tool_result","content":"   390→   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   391→   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   392→   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n   393→\n   394→11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n   395→    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n   396→    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n   397→    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n   398→    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n   399→    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n   400→\n   401→12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   402→   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   403→   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   404→   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   405→   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n   406→\n   407→12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   408→   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   409→   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   410→   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n   411→\n   412→13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n   413→    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n   414→    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n   415→    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n   416→\n   417→14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   418→   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   419→   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   420→   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   421→   overcounting.\n   422→\n   423→14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n   424→    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets\n   425→    the relevant data to seed state via API calls. This is a writing-time requirement, not\n   426→    a fixTests concern. Data contamination accounted for 54% of all test failures in one\n   427→    observed session (62.9% test isolation score). Do not wait for contamination to occur\n   428→    before adding cleanup — add it proactively from the start.\n   429→\n   430→15. **Use `test.describe.serial` as the default for all spec files.** Since all tests share\n   431→    a database branch, parallel execution within a spec file causes data-contamination\n   432→    failures. Serial execution should be the default. Only use `fullyParallel` when tests\n   433→    are provably independent (no shared mutable state).\n   434→\n   435→16. **Shared test utility for seed data reset.** When the same API-based reset logic is\n   436→    needed across multiple spec files (e.g., `resetPayments()`, `resetOrderStatuses()`),\n   437→    extract it to a shared test utility file (e.g., `tests/helpers/reset.ts`). This\n   438→    reduces boilerplate and ensures consistent cleanup across all spec files. The\n   439→    `destructive-test-reordering` pattern was applied identically across 4 spec files in\n   440→    one session — a shared `resetDatabase(resource)` helper would have prevented this\n   441→    duplication.\n   442→\n   443→17. **Single source of truth for seed data expectations.** Seed data counts and values\n   444→    used in test assertions MUST be derived from a single shared constant or queried from\n   445→    the API at test time — never hardcoded independently in multiple spec files. When\n   446→    multiple workers modify the same test files, independent seed data assumptions drift\n   447→    and cause recurring seed-data-mismatch failures. In one session, the same\n   448→    seed-data-mismatch appeared in 3 separate logs because the fix from the first log\n   449→    was overridden by a later worker.\n   450→\n   451→18. **Audit `deleteAll*` helpers for paginated API responses.** When test utilities call\n   452→    `deleteAll` endpoints that return paginated responses (e.g., `{ items: [...], total: N }`),\n   453→    they MUST destructure the response correctly: `const { items } = await response.json()`.\n   454→    Using the raw response object as an array (iterating over `{ items, total }` instead of\n   455→    `items`) silently fails to delete anything, corrupting database state for subsequent tests.\n   456→    This was the direct cause of member table corruption in one observed session.\n   457→\n   458→19. **Reseed tables in FK dependency order.** When reseeding multiple tables in `beforeEach`\n   459→    hooks, respect foreign key constraints by seeding parent tables before child tables. For\n   460→    example: `instructors → members → bookings` (members reference instructors, bookings\n   461→    reference members). Calling reseed endpoints in the wrong order causes FK constraint\n   462→    violations that look like application bugs but are actually test infrastructure issues.\n   463→\n   464→## Pre-Commit Checklist for New Spec Files\n   465→\n   466→Before committing a new spec file, verify:\n   467→- No test creates, modifies, or deletes shared data without cleanup or isolation.\n   468→- Destructive tests (delete-all, empty-state) are in `test.describe.serial` at the end.\n   469→- Status transition tests include `beforeEach` state resets.\n   470→- Created records use unique identifiers (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`).\n   471→- Count assertions use flexible matching (`toBeGreaterThan`) or relative counts.\n   472→\n   473→## JourneyQA / Batch Test Isolation\n   474→\n   475→When running multiple spec files in a single batch (e.g., JourneyQA tasks), each spec file\n   476→must have full database isolation. Running multiple spec files against a single Neon branch\n   477→causes cross-spec data contamination — one batch run caused 19 test failures from shared\n   478→state. Isolation strategies:\n   479→\n   480→1. **Per-spec Neon branches**: Each spec file should get its own ephemeral Neon branch.\n   481→2. **Sequential with resets**: Run specs sequentially with a full database reset (truncate +\n   482→   re-seed) between each spec file.\n   483→3. **Never share a single branch across parallel spec files**: This is the most common source\n   484→   of batch contamination.\n   485→4. **JourneyQA URL validation**: Journey tests against deployed apps MUST use the `/api/`\n   486→   prefix for API calls, not `/.netlify/functions/api/`. The Netlify redirect rewrites `/api/`\n   487→   to `/.netlify/functions/`, so using the full `/.netlify/functions/api/` path results in\n   488→   double-prefixed URLs that return HTML instead of JSON.\n   489→5. **JourneyQA dynamic ID discovery**: JourneyQA tests MUST dynamically discover entity IDs"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ddd60a9-1f50-46d1-9553-9d4e1aeb2461","uuid":"0b582bd3-b184-4f79-b125-a96af043473d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/build/testing.md","content":"   block should verify its preconditions rather than assuming state from prior tests. For\n   example, check that the expected number of rows exists before performing add/delete\n   operations, rather than relying on a previous test's side effects.\n\n11. **Password-change tests must restore original credentials.** Any spec file that\n    changes a user's password MUST restore the original password in `afterAll` or use a\n    dedicated test user account. Password mutations cascade into 18+ failures across other\n    spec files (e.g., personal-info, messages) that rely on the original credentials for\n    API authentication and `beforeEach` cleanup. This is the highest-blast-radius\n    data-contamination pattern observed — a single password change can break 3+ spec files.\n\n12. **Navigate before `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`**. Tests that use `page.evaluate(fetch(...))`\n   for API-driven setup in `beforeEach` or `beforeAll` MUST call `page.goto()` first to\n   navigate to the app. `fetch()` with relative URLs fails at `about:blank` because there\n   is no base URL to resolve against. Always ensure the page has navigated before making\n   fetch calls via `page.evaluate`.\n\n12. **Weekend-safe seed data.** Seed data must include entries for the current day\n   regardless of day-of-week. Use relative date calculations (e.g., `new Date()`) rather\n   than hardcoded weekday dates. Tests that rely on \"today's appointments\" or similar\n   day-specific queries will fail on weekends if seed data only contains weekday entries.\n\n13. **Use click-based interaction for custom dropdowns.** When the UI uses custom dropdown\n    components (non-native `<select>`), tests must use click-based interaction patterns\n    (`click trigger → click option`), not `page.selectOption()`. Assertions should use\n    `getAttribute('data-value')` instead of `toHaveValue()`.\n\n14. **Validate `data-testid` prefix selectors.** When using `[data-testid^=\"prefix-\"]`\n   selectors, verify that container/wrapper elements don't also match the prefix. A selector\n   like `[data-testid^=\"route-stop-\"]` will match both list items and the container if\n   named `route-stop-list`. Use `:not()` exclusions or more specific selectors to avoid\n   overcounting.\n\n14. **Mandatory beforeEach cleanup in ALL spec files that modify data.** Every spec file\n    that creates, updates, or deletes records MUST include a `beforeEach` hook that resets\n    the relevant data to seed state via API calls. This is a writing-time requirement, not\n    a fixTests concern. Data contamination accounted for 54% of all test failures in one\n    observed session (62.9% test isolation score). Do not wait for contamination to occur\n    before adding cleanup — add it proactively from the start.\n\n15. **Use `test.describe.serial` as the default for all spec files.** Since all tests share\n    a database branch, parallel execution within a spec file causes data-contamination\n    failures. Serial execution should be the default. Only use `fullyParallel` when tests\n    are provably independent (no shared mutable state).\n\n16. **Shared test utility for seed data reset.** When the same API-based reset logic is\n    needed across multiple spec files (e.g., `resetPayments()`, `resetOrderStatuses()`),\n    extract it to a shared test utility file (e.g., `tests/helpers/reset.ts`). This\n    reduces boilerplate and ensures consistent cleanup across all spec files. The\n    `destructive-test-reordering` pattern was applied identically across 4 spec files in\n    one session — a shared `resetDatabase(resource)` helper would have prevented this\n    duplication.\n\n17. **Single source of truth for seed data expectations.** Seed data counts and values\n    used in test assertions MUST be derived from a single shared constant or queried from\n    the API at test time — never hardcoded independently in multiple spec files. When\n    multiple workers modify the same test files, independent seed data assumptions drift\n    and cause recurring seed-data-mismatch failures. In one session, the same\n    seed-data-mismatch appeared in 3 separate logs because the fix from the first log\n    was overridden by a later worker.\n\n18. **Audit `deleteAll*` helpers for paginated API responses.** When test utilities call\n    `deleteAll` endpoints that return paginated responses (e.g., `{ items: [...], total: N }`),\n    they MUST destructure the response correctly: `const { items } = await response.json()`.\n    Using the raw response object as an array (iterating over `{ items, total }` instead of\n    `items`) silently fails to delete anything, corrupting database state for subsequent tests.\n    This was the direct cause of member table corruption in one observed session.\n\n19. **Reseed tables in FK dependency order.** When reseeding multiple tables in `beforeEach`\n    hooks, respect foreign key constraints by seeding parent tables before child tables. For\n    example: `instructors → members → bookings` (members reference instructors, bookings\n    reference members). Calling reseed endpoints in the wrong order causes FK constraint\n    violations that look like application bugs but are actually test infrastructure issues.\n\n## Pre-Commit Checklist for New Spec Files\n\nBefore committing a new spec file, verify:\n- No test creates, modifies, or deletes shared data without cleanup or isolation.\n- Destructive tests (delete-all, empty-state) are in `test.describe.serial` at the end.\n- Status transition tests include `beforeEach` state resets.\n- Created records use unique identifiers (`Date.now()`, `crypto.randomUUID()`).\n- Count assertions use flexible matching (`toBeGreaterThan`) or relative counts.\n\n## JourneyQA / Batch Test Isolation\n\nWhen running multiple spec files in a single batch (e.g., JourneyQA tasks), each spec file\nmust have full database isolation. Running multiple spec files against a single Neon branch\ncauses cross-spec data contamination — one batch run caused 19 test failures from shared\nstate. Isolation strategies:\n\n1. **Per-spec Neon branches**: Each spec file should get its own ephemeral Neon branch.\n2. **Sequential with resets**: Run specs sequentially with a full database reset (truncate +\n   re-seed) between each spec file.\n3. **Never share a single branch across parallel spec files**: This is the most common source\n   of batch contamination.\n4. **JourneyQA URL validation**: Journey tests against deployed apps MUST use the `/api/`\n   prefix for API calls, not `/.netlify/functions/api/`. The Netlify redirect rewrites `/api/`\n   to `/.netlify/functions/`, so using the full `/.netlify/functions/api/` path results in\n   double-prefixed URLs that return HTML instead of JSON.\n5. **JourneyQA dynamic ID discovery**: JourneyQA tests MUST dynamically discover entity IDs","numLines":100,"startLine":390,"totalLines":924}}}
